# Cold-start API sheet (for the user's review before ms33): op-local redo over lifted per-step state

The concepts are the user's (op-owned guards with a local redo; lifting global state into op inputs; closure "not relaxing
any existing safety"). This sheet names the API that carries them into core. Every item is opt-in; with none of it set, core
behaves exactly as ms32 (whose default redoes library bodies; the adapters refuse them through learnfast's no_lib_redo today). The only callers are the vLLM integration adapters (r27 armV_r27, MiMo armV, integration
armV_stock_fast_m4).

## 1. `HostTraceReplay(fn, ..., redo_policy="all")`
- `"all"` (default): unchanged. Core today redoes any op at a redispatch, library bodies included (a non-ATen operator or a
  dispatch unit runs its Python body in the redo).
- `"refuse"`: a redispatch never redoes a library-body op; the call retraces. This is what the vLLM adapters do today by
  importing learnfast's no_lib_redo (a library body reading per-step state from a holder would bake the current call's value
  into the entry); it moves into core under this name.
- `"inputs_only"`: such an op may redo only if
  (a) statically, every free symbol of its records (launches, allocations, the guards it owns) is one of its own call's
      inputs, its own allocations', a `lifted` position's (item 2), or (with item 4) a declared storage's constant; and
  (b) on its first redispatch, a confirmation (the op redone once at the trace call's own values, evaluated from the
      trace call's arguments, no hint reads) reproduces its recorded launches exactly, launch for launch (function, grid,
      block, smem, every slot, every root), and records no guard beyond the trace's except ones over its own symbols.
      Cached per traced op (variant x op index), not per operator name.
  Otherwise it refuses as "refuse" does.
  A failing guard that pins a lifted symbol (s == c) refuses, unless every record of it was made inside an ATen op's C++
  symbolic evaluation within the op (a kernel-internal decision, re-evaluated by the redo). Anything unproven refuses,
  labelled ("redo_policy: <op> reads <symbol>, not an input" / "confirmation: <first differing field>").

## 2. `HostTraceReplay(fn, ..., lifted=None)`
- `None` (default): no lifted positions.
- A `range` or sequence of positions in the call's flat arguments that carry per-step state the integration lifted out of
  its holders (vLLM: the step's attention / GDN metadata tensors and lifted ints). Under `inputs_only`, their symbols count
  as an op's inputs; they are what `redo_context` installs. Declared by the caller, never inferred.

## 3. `HostTraceReplay(fn, ..., redo_context=None)`
- `None` (default): an op whose records read a lifted symbol refuses ("reads lifted state; no redo_context").
- `Callable[[RedoCall], ContextManager]`. Core calls it only around the redo of an op whose records read a lifted symbol
  (lazy), with `RedoCall(args, func, rank)`:
  - `args`: the call's flat arguments, with the redo's symbolic stand-ins at the `lifted` positions (and at every
    position the op reads), the variant's own objects elsewhere;
  - `func`: the op's OpOverload;
  - `rank`: the op's index among the tape's ops of the same `func` (vLLM's MoE ops find their layer by such a counter).
  The context manager makes the integration's holders hold `args`' stand-ins while the redo runs, and restores them on
  exit (vLLM: `_md_rebuild` + `override_forward_context`, and the MoE counter at `rank`). A body that reads a copy instead
  of the holder fails (a) or (b) and refuses.

## 4. `torch.cuda._host_trace.close_over(modules=(), tensors=()) -> Closure`
- Declares storages fixed by address (vLLM bakes the same pointers into its own graphs): every parameter and buffer of
  `modules`, every tensor held as one of their attributes (and lists of them: KV caches, mamba state, pre-made views),
  and `tensors` (persistent runner buffers). `Closure.release()` undeclares.
- In a trace or redo, a tensor on a declared storage gets a constant base; its offset / sizes / strides trace as usual. A
  declared object (a pre-made view in module state) traces with its layout guarded. Any other plain tensor on a declared
  storage (a per-step view the runner made) must arrive as an argument or the read declines; its layout is never baked.
- Invalidation, no per-call check: Module.__setattr__ / register_parameter / register_buffer on declared modules, and
  `.data =` / set_ / resize_ / in-place metadata ops on declared tensors, mark every variant that closed over them stale
  (its next call retraces). In-place value writes (weights, KV) stay valid, as in vLLM.
- Default: nothing declared.

## 5. `torch.cuda._host_trace.redo_resolves_arguments` (exists, RD5; semantics narrowed and extended)
- Resolution becomes by object identity only: a plain tensor a redo's body reaches that IS one of the call's tensor
  arguments becomes that argument's stand-in (no storage / view equality, no tie-break; anything else declines). It now
  also applies at the TorchFunction level (before autograd), at the Triton launch intercept and in record_launch.
- Default unchanged (off).

## Not API (internal, with tests)
- CuTe `_roots_of` also searches a redo's outer roots.
- Guard records carry whether they were made inside an ATen op's C++ symbolic evaluation (for the pin rule in 1).
- A redo runs under the trace's builtin min / max handling (trace_builtin_minmax).

## Stays in the integration (labelled overlays, not core)
- fla_symlen (flash-linear-attention chunked ops use `.shape[0]` instead of `len()`; `len()` must return a Python int).
- The adapters turning 1-4 on, and sections on with eager-order memory.

Dropped: cross-layer template key-out (tpl_layer): on ms32 it costs more than it saves (27B ready 146.0 s without it vs 179.2 s
with it). Held for the user: the unread unpinned CPU argument kind (X1, ~30 s of warm-up and ~55 s to steady on 27B).
